# What never belongs in the Python build context.
#
# Two of these entries are load-bearing rather than tidy:
#
#   * the env files. They hold provider keys and a database password, and a
#     ``COPY . .`` would bake them into an image layer where they survive every
#     later ``rm`` and travel with every ``docker push``. The container gets its
#     environment from Compose at *run* time instead — which is also what
#     ``graph_library/env_files.py`` calls the normal orchestrated case.
#   * ``frontend/``. The backend image has no use for 200 MB of ``node_modules``
#     and a ``dist/`` bundle, and the frontend has its own context and its own
#     ``.dockerignore``.

.env
.env.*
!.env.example

frontend/

.git/
.github/
.idea/
.vscode/
.claude/

__pycache__/
*.py[cod]
.pytest_cache/
.ruff_cache/
.mypy_cache/
*.egg-info/
build/
dist/
.langgraph_api/

.venv/
venv/

Dockerfile
docker-compose*.yml
.dockerignore

*.log
!sample_logs/*.log
.DS_Store
